Session 1 Reflection

Management of AI Products
Personal Learning Journal – Session 1

The way I see the role of a Product Manager has been changed by this session. Previously I thought that a Product Manager's main job was to collect requirements and act as a coordinator between the business 
and engineering teams, but the professor referred to the Product Manager as the "CEO of the product"; this remark hit me since it shifts the emphasis from coordination to ownership.

I can immediately see the connection with my present position at work. Even though my job title is connected with automation, I frequently work with HR stakeholders, RPA developers, architects, 
and business partners. There are several instances in which I have had to make decisions even though the development team doesn't report directly to me. 
I have already been engaging in some of the activities associated with product management, even if I haven't formally referred to it as such.

There was also the idea of accountability which made me consider matters in a different way. The professor pointed out that a Product Manager is responsible for both the success and the failure of the product.
At first, I found this to be not fair since there are many factors which are beyond the control of the Product Manager. However, after giving it some thought, I realized that ownership 
doesn't imply having to do all the work oneself; it just means making sure that the product achieves the result it is meant to. 
I believe this way of thinking is very important, particularly since I hope to move into AI Product Management in the future.

The idea that product management is a "thankless job" was interesting too; I have come across similar situations in connection with automation projects in which users at first appreciated the solution but later asked for more features without realizing the effort that had already been put in. The professor's reply—that we should focus on measuring progress rather than expecting appreciation—was sensible and altered the way I saw things. It made me aware that the act of creating something valuable should be satisfying in itself.

I also took some time to think about my own product, PrepLens AI. Up to now, my main emphasis has been on developing features and incorporating AI models. However, after this session I came to the conclusion that I should spend more time considering whether each feature actually addresses a real user problem. Rather than asking myself "What can AI do?", I should be asking "What problem am I solving for job seekers?"

I learned that it's important to record personal reflections rather than just preparing summaries. At first, I believed that keeping weekly notes would amount to extra work, but I now regard them as a means of capturing my own thought process. After looking back on them a few months later, I will be able to see how my product management mindset has changed.

In summary, this session has strengthened my long-term aim in my career. I would like to make the shift from being a person who develops automation solutions to one who has full responsibility for AI products. The ideas presented in this session give a solid basis for that kind of transition.

Key takeaway for myself:

A Product Manager does not act as a co-ordinator; rather, the Product Manager is responsible for the problem, the decisions, and finally the outcome.
